bg.2018.2.6.reviewed_statement:
On a planet there are $M$ countries and $N$ cities. Two-way roads have been built between some of the cities. It is known that:

(1) every country has at least three cities;

(2) from every city in any country there are roads to at least half of the remaining cities in that country;

(3) from every city there is exactly one road to a city in some other country;

(4) between the cities of two different countries there are at most two roads;

(5) if two countries have together fewer than $2M$ cities, then there is at least one road between these two countries.

Prove that a traveller can visit at least $M + \frac{N}{2}$ cities, travelling only along the roads, and finally return to the city from which he started.

it.2013.0.1.reviewed_statement:
A model car is tested on some closed circuits $600$ metres long, made up of flat stretches and of uphill or downhill stretches. All uphill and downhill stretches have the same slope. The tests reveal some curious facts:

(a) the speed of the car depends only on whether it is going uphill, on the flat or downhill; calling these three speeds $v_s$, $v_p$, $v_d$ respectively, we have $v_s < v_p < v_d$;

(b) $v_s$, $v_p$ and $v_d$, expressed in metres per second, are integers;

(c) however the circuit is made up (with more or fewer climbs and descents), the time needed to complete it is always $50$ seconds.

Find all possible values of $v_s$, $v_p$, $v_d$.

it.2008.0.1.reviewed_statement:
Let $ABCDEFGHILMN$ be a regular dodecagon. Let $P$ be the intersection of the diagonals $AF$ and $DH$. Let $S$ be the circle through $A$ and $H$ that is congruent to the circumcircle of the dodecagon and distinct from it. Prove that:

(a) $P$ lies on $S$;

(b) the center of $S$ lies on the diagonal $HN$;

(c) the length of $PE$ equals the side of the dodecagon.

ee.2004.0.5.reviewed_statement:
Let $n$ and $c$ be coprime positive integers. For any integer $i$, let $i'$ denote the remainder of $ci$ upon division by $n$. Let $A_0A_1 \ldots A_{n-1}$ be a regular $n$-gon. Prove that

a) if the segments $A_iA_j$ and $A_kA_l$ are parallel, then the segments $A_{i'}A_{j'}$ and $A_{k'}A_{l'}$ are also parallel;

b) if the segments $A_iA_j$ and $A_kA_l$ are perpendicular, then the segments $A_{i'}A_{j'}$ and $A_{k'}A_{l'}$ are also perpendicular.

frt.2019.4.10.reviewed_statement:
Let $\mathcal{C}$ be a circle of radius $1$ and let $T$ be a real number. A set of triangles is called $T$-daring if it satisfies the following three conditions:
- the vertices of each triangle lie on $\mathcal{C}$;
- the triangles have pairwise disjoint interiors (but two triangles may share a side or a vertex);
- each triangle has perimeter strictly greater than $T$.
Find all real numbers $T$ such that, for every integer $n\ge 1$, there exists a $T$-daring set containing exactly $n$ triangles.

hkt.2014.1.5.reviewed_statement:
Find the largest integer $n$ satisfying the following conditions:

(i) $n^2$ can be expressed as the difference of two consecutive cubes;

(ii) $2n + 79$ is a perfect square.

hkt.2015.1.2.reviewed_statement:
Find the maximum number of sets which simultaneously satisfy the following $3$ conditions:

(i) any of the sets consists of $4$ elements;

(ii) any two different sets have exactly $2$ common elements;

(iii) no two elements are common to all the sets.

si.2012.0.4.reviewed_statement:
The mole Črt has $6$ chambers in his burrow, numbered with the numbers from $1$ to $6$. Between some of them Črt has dug tunnels, so that from every chamber he can crawl through some tunnels to every other chamber. No two tunnels intersect. Every tunnel begins in some chamber, ends in some other chamber (different from the starting one), and does not pass through any chamber in between. Two chambers that are directly connected by a tunnel are called adjacent. List the pairs of adjacent chambers if the following holds:
- if Črt crawls through exactly three (not necessarily distinct) tunnels, he can get from chamber $1$ to chambers $1$ and $6$;
- if Črt crawls through exactly three (not necessarily distinct) tunnels, he cannot get from chamber $1$ to chamber $2$;
- if Črt crawls through exactly four (not necessarily distinct) tunnels, he cannot get from chamber $1$ to chamber $6$;
- if Črt crawls through exactly two tunnels, he cannot get from chamber $1$ to chamber $5$.

lv.2009.0.4.reviewed_statement:
On a certain planet $2009$ languages are used. Can a system of dictionaries be created so that the following $3$ properties hold simultaneously:
(a) each dictionary allows one to translate from one language into some other, but not in the opposite direction;
(b) if there is a dictionary that allows one to translate from some language $A$ into some other language $B$, then there is no dictionary that allows one to translate from $B$ into $A$;
(c) from every language into every other one can translate using either one or two dictionaries? (Repeated translations are allowed, for example, from $A$ into $B$ and further from $B$ into $C$.)

lv.2015.0.5.reviewed_statement:
In the Votivapu language all words consist only of the two letters $a$ and $b$. Every word can be obtained from the word "$a$" by repeatedly applying the following three rules:
1) appending the letter $b$ at the end of the word;
2) appending the word itself at its end;
3) replacing three consecutive letters $a$ in the word by one letter $b$.
Are the following words in the Votivapu language: (a) $abbababab$; (b) $baabaabaa$?

eet.2005.1.2.reviewed_statement:
On the planet Automory, there are infinitely many inhabitants. Every Automorian loves exactly one Automorian and honours exactly one Automorian. Additionally, the following can be noticed:
- each Automorian is loved by some Automorian;
- if Automorian $A$ loves Automorian $B$, then also all Automorians honouring $A$ love $B$;
- if Automorian $A$ honours Automorian $B$, then also all Automorians loving $A$ honour $B$.
Is it correct to claim that every Automorian honours and loves the same Automorian?

cyt.2016.2.5.reviewed_statement:
Someone fits four new tyres to his car and buys a new spare tyre. All five tyres are identical. If we know that the rear tyres of the car wear out after $42000$ km while the front ones wear out after $58000$ km, find:
(a) the maximum distance in km that the car can travel with these five tyres;
(b) propose a way of using the tyres so that the car travels the maximum distance using all five tyres.

ukt.1989.1.2.reviewed_statement:
$ABC$ is a triangle; $D, E, F$ are the feet of the perpendiculars from $A, B, C$ respectively to the opposite sides; $D', E', F'$ are the midpoints of $EF, FD, DE$ respectively.
The line $E'F'$ meets $CA$ at $Q$ and $AB$ at $R'$;
$F'D'$ meets $AB$ at $R$ and $BC$ at $P'$;
$D'E'$ meets $BC$ at $P$ and $CA$ at $Q'$.
Prove that
(i) $QR' = RP' = PQ'$;
(ii) $P, P', Q, Q', R, R'$ lie on a circle.

ukt.1997.1.2.reviewed_statement:
Determine whether or not there exist two disjoint infinite sets $\mathcal{A}$ and $\mathcal{B}$ of points in the plane satisfying the following two conditions:
(a) no three points in $\mathcal{A} \cup \mathcal{B}$ are collinear, and the distance between any two points in $\mathcal{A} \cup \mathcal{B}$ is at least $1$;
(b) there is a point of $\mathcal{A}$ in any triangle whose vertices are in $\mathcal{B}$, and there is a point of $\mathcal{B}$ in any triangle whose vertices are in $\mathcal{A}$.

ukt.2004.1.3.reviewed_statement:
Let $\mathbb{R}^+$ be the set of positive real numbers. Find all functions $f : \mathbb{R}^+ \to \mathbb{R}^+$ that satisfy the following conditions:
(i) $f(xyz) + f(x) + f(y) + f(z) = f(\sqrt{xy})f(\sqrt{yz})f(\sqrt{zx})$ for all $x, y, z \in \mathbb{R}^+$;
(ii) $f(x) < f(y)$ for all $1 \le x < y$.

ukt.2004.3.7.reviewed_statement:
Find all nondecreasing functions $f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ such that
(a) $f(0) = 0$, $f(1) = 1$;
(b) $f(a) + f(b) = f(a)f(b) + f(a + b - ab)$ for all real numbers $a, b$ such that $a < 1 < b$.

ukt.2004.3.9.reviewed_statement:
Each positive integer $a$ (written in base $10$ notation) undergoes the following procedure in order to obtain the number $d = d(a)$:
(a) move the last digit of $a$ to the first position to obtain the number $b$;
(b) square $b$ to obtain the number $c$;
(c) move the first digit of $c$ to the end to obtain the number $d$.
(For example, for $a = 2003$, we get $b = 3200$, $c = 10240000$, and $d = 02400001 = 2400001 = d(2003)$.)
Find all numbers $a$ for which $d(a) = a^2$.

ukt.2007.6.17.reviewed_statement:
Find all pairs of functions $f, g : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ which satisfy:
(a) $f(x \cdot g(y+1)) + y = x \cdot f(y) + f(x + g(y))$, for all $x, y \in \mathbb{R}$;
(b) $f(0) + g(0) = 0$.

ukt.2011.4.10.reviewed_statement:
Find all functions $f : \mathbb{Z} \to \mathbb{Z}$ such that:
(a) $f(x + f(x + 2y)) = f(2x) + f(2y)$ for all integers $x, y$;
(b) $f(0) = 2$.

nl.2016.0.3.reviewed_statement:
Find all possible triples $(a,b,c)$ of positive integers with the following properties:
- $\gcd(a,b)=\gcd(a,c)=\gcd(b,c)=1$;
- $a$ is a divisor of $a+b+c$;
- $b$ is a divisor of $a+b+c$;
- $c$ is a divisor of $a+b+c$.

(Here $\gcd(x,y)$ is the greatest common divisor of $x$ and $y$.)

nl.2016.0.5.reviewed_statement:
Bas has coloured each of the positive integers. He had several colours at his disposal. His colouring satisfies the following requirements:
- each odd integer is coloured blue;
- each integer $n$ has the same colour as $4n$;
- each integer $n$ has the same colour as at least one of the integers $n+2$ and $n+4$.

Prove that Bas has coloured all integers blue.

nlt.2011.2.10.reviewed_statement:
Find all triples $(a,b,c)$ of positive integers with $a+b+c=10$ such that there exist $a$ red, $b$ blue and $c$ green points (all distinct) in the plane with the following properties:
- for each red point and each blue point we consider the distance between these two points; the sum of all these distances is $37$;
- for each green point and each red point we consider the distance between these two points; the sum of all these distances is $30$;
- for each blue point and each green point we consider the distance between these two points; the sum of all these distances is $1$.

nlt.2019.2.5.reviewed_statement:
At a secondary school, every class has an odd number of pupils. Furthermore, every pupil has a best friend (possibly from another class). Everyone is the best friend of his or her best friend. For the city trip, every pupil goes to Rome or to Paris. Prove that the pupils can be divided over the two destinations such that

(i) every pupil goes on the city trip together with his best friend;

(ii) for every class, the absolute difference between the number of pupils going to Rome and the number of pupils going to Paris is equal to $1$.

nlt.2019.3.11.reviewed_statement:
Find all functions $f:\mathbb{Z}\to\mathbb{Z}$ satisfying the following two conditions:

(i) for all integers $x$ we have $f(f(x))=x$;

(ii) for all integers $x$ and $y$ such that $x+y$ is odd we have $f(x)+f(y)\ge x+y$.

ch.2006.2.10.reviewed_statement:
Decide whether there exists an integer $n > 1$ with the following properties:
(a) $n$ is not a prime number;
(b) $a^n - a$ is divisible by $n$ for all integers $a$.

ch.2009.1.1.reviewed_statement:
Let $P$ be a regular hexagon. For a point $A$ let $d_1 \le d_2 \le \dots \le d_6$ be the distances from $A$ to the six vertices of $P$, ordered by size. Find the locus of all points $A$ in the interior or on the boundary of $P$ such that
(a) $d_3$ takes the smallest possible value;
(b) $d_4$ takes the smallest possible value.

ch.2020.2.6.reviewed_statement:
Let $n \ge 2$ be an integer. Consider the following game: initially, $k$ stones are distributed among the $n^2$ squares of an $n \times n$ chessboard. A move consists of choosing a square containing at least as many stones as the number of its adjacent squares (two squares are adjacent if they share a common edge) and moving one stone from this square to each of its adjacent squares. Determine all positive integers $k$ such that:
(a) there is an initial configuration with $k$ stones such that no move is possible;
(b) there is an initial configuration with $k$ stones such that an infinite sequence of moves is possible.

cht.2009.1.1.reviewed_statement:
Let $GERMANYISHOT$ be a regular dodecagon. The lines $GN$ and $MI$ intersect at the point $P$. Prove that
(a) the circumcircle of triangle $GIP$ is as large as the circumcircle of $GERMANYISHOT$;
(b) the segment $PA$ is as long as a side of $GERMANYISHOT$.

cht.2013.3.9.reviewed_statement:
Find all functions $f: \mathbb{R}_{>0} \times \mathbb{R}_{>0} \to \mathbb{R}_{>0}$ such that the following two conditions are satisfied:
(a) $f\left(f\left(\frac{x}{y}, y\right), z\right) = x \cdot f(1, z)$ for all $x, y, z \in \mathbb{R}_{>0}$;
(b) the function $g: \mathbb{R}_{>0} \to \mathbb{R}_{>0}$ defined by $g(x) = f(x, x)$ for all $x \in \mathbb{R}_{>0}$ is monotonic.

hk.2004.0.4.reviewed_statement:
Let $S = \{1, 2, \dots, 100\}$. Determine the number of functions $f : S \to S$ satisfying the following conditions.

(i) $f(1) = 1$;

(ii) $f$ is bijective (i.e. for every $y$ in $S$, the equation $f(x) = y$ has exactly one solution);

(iii) $f(n) = f(g(n)) f(h(n))$ for every $n$ in $S$.

Here $g(n)$ and $h(n)$ denote the uniquely determined positive integers such that $g(n) \le h(n)$, $g(n)h(n) = n$ and $h(n) - g(n)$ is as small as possible. (For instance, $g(80) = 8$, $h(80) = 10$ and $g(81) = h(81) = 9$.)

hk.2016.0.2.reviewed_statement:
Let $k$ be a positive integer. Find the number of nonnegative integers $n$ less than or equal to $10^k$ satisfying the following conditions:

(i) $n$ is divisible by $3$;

(ii) Each decimal digit of $n$ is one of the digits $2, 0, 1$ or $7$.

hkt.1997.2.6.reviewed_statement:
For a positive integer $n$, let $f(n)$ be the largest $k$ such that $2^k$ divides $n$ and $g(n)$ be the sum of the digits of the binary representation of $n$. Prove that for any positive integers $n$,

(i) $f(n!) = n - g(n)$;

(ii) $4$ divides $\binom{2n}{n} = \frac{(2n)!}{n!\,n!}$ if and only if $n$ is not a power of $2$.

hkt.1998.2.9.reviewed_statement:
Let $f$ be a function with the following properties:

(i) $f(n)$ is defined for every positive integer $n$;

(ii) $f(n)$ is a positive integer;

(iii) $f(f(m) + f(n)) = m + n$ for all $m$ and $n$.

Find $f(1997)$.

hkt.2008.1.3.reviewed_statement:
Let $a$, $b$ and $c$ be the sides of a triangle and $p = a + b + c$. Show that

(a) $\frac{a}{b + c - a} + \frac{b}{c + a - b} + \frac{c}{a + b - c} \ge 3$;

(b) $a^k + b^k > \frac{c^k}{2^{k-1}}$ for $k = 2, 3, 4, \dots$;

(c) $a^k + b^k + c^k \ge \frac{p^k}{3^{k-1}}$ for $k = 2, 3, 4, \dots$.

hkt.2021.2.10.reviewed_statement:
Does there exist a nonzero polynomial $P(x)$ with integer coefficients satisfying both of the following conditions?

(i) $P(x)$ has no rational root;

(ii) For every positive integer $n$, there exists an integer $m$ such that $n$ divides $P(m)$.

ro.2018.0.3.reviewed_statement:
Let $n \in \mathbb{N}^*$, $n \ge 2$. Prove that, for any complex numbers $a_1, a_2, \dots, a_n$ and $b_1, b_2, \dots, b_n$, the following statements are equivalent:

a) $\displaystyle\sum_{k=1}^{n} |z - a_k|^2 \le \sum_{k=1}^{n} |z - b_k|^2$ for every $z \in \mathbb{C}$;

b) $\displaystyle\sum_{k=1}^{n} a_k = \sum_{k=1}^{n} b_k$ and $\displaystyle\sum_{k=1}^{n} |a_k|^2 \le \sum_{k=1}^{n} |b_k|^2$.

rot.2019.1.4.reviewed_statement:
Let $m$ and $n$ be two positive integers and let $A_1, \dots, A_m$ be sets of positive integers such that:

(1) $A_i$ and $A_j$ are disjoint for all distinct indices $i$ and $j$;

(2) $|A_i| = n$, $i = 1, \dots, m$;

(3) For every index $i$, no element of $A_i$ is divisible by any element of $A_{i+1}$, where indices are taken modulo $m$.

Determine the maximum number of ordered pairs $(a, b)$, where $a$ and $b$ are elements of different $A_i$'s and $b$ is divisible by $a$.

rot.2021.1.2.reviewed_statement:
For each positive integer $n > 1$, denote by $p(n)$ the largest prime factor of $n$. Determine all triples $x, y, z$ of distinct positive integers which simultaneously satisfy the conditions:

(1) $x, y, z$ are in arithmetic progression;

(2) $p(xyz) \le 3$.

lt.2026.0.3.reviewed_statement:
Sets $A$ and $B$ are non-empty subsets of the set $\mathbb{Z}$ of all integers such that:
(i) every integer belongs to at least one of the sets $A$ and $B$;
(ii) for every number $x$ in the set $A$, the number $x-1$ is in the set $B$;
(iii) for every pair $(x,y)$ of (not necessarily distinct) numbers of the set $B$, the number $x+y$ is in the set $A$.
Determine:
(a) at least one number which is guaranteed to be in the set $A$;
(b) all possible such pairs of sets $(A,B)$.

ltt.2011.1.6.reviewed_statement:
Let $m\ge 2$ and $n$ be positive integers. The set $S(m,n)$ consists of all polynomials $P(x)$ whose coefficients belong to the set $\{0,1,2,\dots,m^2-1\}$ and which satisfy the condition $P(m)=n$. Find the number of elements of the set $S(m,n)$ when
(a) $n=10m$;
(b) $n=m^2$;
(c) $n$ is an arbitrary positive integer.

ltt.2025.1.1.reviewed_statement:
An infinite sequence of positive integers $a_1,a_2,a_3,\dots$ satisfies the following conditions:
(i) $2<a_1<a_2<a_3<\dots$;
(ii) for every term $a_i$ of the sequence, all its divisors greater than $2$ are also terms of this sequence;
(iii) for every pair of terms $(a_i,a_j)$, $i\neq j$, the number $a_ia_j+1$ is also a term of this sequence.
Determine all such sequences.

lv.1994.0.2.reviewed_statement:
The plane is divided into unit squares like a sheet of squared paper; the side length of each square is $1$. A pentagon is drawn whose vertices all lie at vertices of the grid. Prove that:
(a) its area is at least $\frac{3}{2}$;
(b) if the pentagon is convex, then its area is at least $\frac{5}{2}$.

lv.1995.0.2.reviewed_statement:
Prove that for every positive integer $n$:
(a) $1^3+2^3+\dots+n^3$ is divisible by $1+2+\dots+n$;
(b) $1^5+2^5+\dots+n^5$ is divisible by $1+2+\dots+n$.

lv.2001.0.4.reviewed_statement:
Circles $\omega_1$ and $\omega_2$ with centres $O_1$ and $O_2$ respectively intersect at points $A$ and $B$. The rays $O_1B$ and $O_2B$ meet $\omega_2$ and $\omega_1$ again at points $E$ and $F$ respectively. The line through $B$ parallel to $EF$ meets $\omega_1$ and $\omega_2$ again at points $M$ and $N$ respectively. Prove that:
(a) $A$, $O_1$, $O_2$, $E$, $F$ lie on one circle;
(b) $MN=AE+AF$.

lv.2001.0.5.reviewed_statement:
The $10$ volumes of the collected works of Professor Cipariņš stand on a shelf in one row. In one move it is allowed to take any number of adjacent volumes (possibly a single volume) and, keeping them in the same order and without turning them around, put them anywhere on the shelf (at one end, at the other end, or between two volumes remaining on the shelf). Professor Cipariņš wants to rearrange the volumes in reverse order. Find a positive integer $x$ as small as possible and a positive integer $y$ as large as possible with the following properties:
(a) Cipariņš can achieve his goal in $x$ moves;
(b) Cipariņš cannot achieve his goal in $y$ moves.

lv.2002.0.3.reviewed_statement:
There are $1001$ one-lats coins on a table. Each of them may lie either with the coat of arms or with the salmon facing up. $1000$ moves are made. In the $n$-th move ($n=1, 2, 3, \dots, 1000$) one chooses $n$ coins and turns them over. Prove that:
(a) one can achieve that after all the moves have been made, all coins lie with the same side up;
(b) whether the coins will lie with the coat of arms or with the salmon facing up is uniquely determined by the initial position of the coins.

lv.2002.0.4.reviewed_statement:
A point $C$ is an interior point of a segment $AB$. Equilateral triangles $ACB_1$ and $CBA_1$ lie on one side of the line $AB$, and an equilateral triangle $ABC_1$ lies on the other side. The lines $AA_1$ and $BB_1$ meet at a point $S$. Prove that:
(a) $\angle B_1SA=60^\circ$;
(b) the points $S$, $C$ and $C_1$ lie on one line.

lv.2005.0.4.reviewed_statement:
About a sequence of numbers $x_1, x_2, x_3, \dots$ it is known that
$x_1=1$;
$x_{2n}=1+x_n$ for all positive integers $n$;
$x_{2n+1}=\frac{1}{x_{2n}}$ for all positive integers $n$.
(a) Prove that all terms of the sequence are distinct.
(b) Which numbers are terms of this sequence?

lv.2005.0.5.reviewed_statement:
Each vertex of a regular $1000$-gon is coloured white, red or green. In one move it is allowed to choose two adjacent vertices that are coloured in different colours and recolour both of them in the third colour. Prove that:
one can achieve that all vertices are coloured in one and the same colour;
this "final colour" is uniquely determined by the initial colouring.

lv.2013.0.5.reviewed_statement:
A string of letters containing only the letters $a$, $b$ and $c$ is written on a blackboard. The following moves may be performed with this string:
- change the order of the written letters arbitrarily;
- if the fragment $ab$ is written at the end of the string, it may be erased;
- replace a fragment $ba$ with the fragment $aabbcc$;
- replace a fragment $bbc$ with $a$;
- delete any three equal letters written consecutively.
Is it possible, by repeating such moves several times, to obtain the string $aba$ if initially the string written is
1) $abba$;
2) $aabbcabaab$?

lv.2025.0.5.reviewed_statement:
Two polynomials $P(x)=x^2+2$ and $Q(x)=x+1$ are written on a blackboard. In one move Ilmārs chooses two polynomials $a$ and $b$ already written on the blackboard ($a$ and $b$ may also be one and the same polynomial) and writes on the blackboard one of the polynomials $a+b$, $a-b$ or $a\cdot b$ (the result may also be a polynomial of degree zero, which is a number). Can he, by repeating such operations, at some moment succeed in writing on the blackboard the polynomial
(a) $x^3+2$;
(b) $x^4+2$?

eet.1996.0.3.reviewed_statement:
Find all functions $f:\mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R}$ satisfying the following conditions for all $x\in\mathbb{R}$:
(a) $f(x)=-f(-x)$;
(b) $f(x+1)=f(x)+1$;
(c) $f\left(\frac1x\right)=\frac{1}{x^2}f(x)$, if $x\ne 0$.

eet.2001.1.1.reviewed_statement:
Consider on the coordinate plane all rectangles whose
(i) vertices have integer coordinates;
(ii) edges are parallel to coordinate axes;
(iii) area is $2^k$, where $k=0,1,2,\dots$.
Is it possible to color all points with integer coordinates in two colors so that no such rectangle has all its vertices of the same color?

eet.2003.1.3.reviewed_statement:
Let $\mathbb{N}$ be the set of all non-negative integers and for each $n\in\mathbb{N}$ denote $n'=n+1$. The function $A:\mathbb{N}^3\to\mathbb{N}$ is defined as follows:
(i) $A(0,m,n)=m'$ for all $m,n\in\mathbb{N}$;
(ii) $A(k',0,n)=\begin{cases} n, & \text{if } k=0,\\ 0, & \text{if } k=1,\\ 1, & \text{if } k>1\end{cases}$ for all $k,n\in\mathbb{N}$;
(iii) $A(k',m',n)=A(k,A(k',m,n),n)$ for all $k,m,n\in\mathbb{N}$.
Compute $A(5,3,2)$.

eet.2010.1.1.reviewed_statement:
For arbitrary positive integers $a$, $b$, denote $a\ominus b=\frac{a-b}{\gcd(a,b)}$. Let $n$ be a positive integer. Prove that the following conditions are equivalent:
(i) $\gcd(n,n\ominus m)=1$ for every positive integer $m<n$;
(ii) $n=p^k$ where $p$ is a prime number and $k$ is a non-negative integer.

eet.2011.1.3.reviewed_statement:
Does there exist an operation $*$ on the set of all integers such that the following conditions hold simultaneously:
(1) for any integers $x$, $y$, $z$, $(x*y)*z=x*(y*z)$;
(2) for any integers $x$ and $y$, $x*x*y=y*x*x=y$?

eet.2012.1.2.reviewed_statement:
For a given positive integer $n$ one has to choose positive integers $a_0, a_1, \dots$ so that the following conditions hold:
(1) $a_i=a_{i+n}$ for any $i$;
(2) $a_i$ is not divisible by $n$ for any $i$;
(3) $a_{i+a_i}$ is divisible by $a_i$ for any $i$.
For which positive integers $n>1$ is this possible only if the numbers $a_0, a_1, \dots$ are all equal?

eet.2017.2.7.reviewed_statement:
Let $n$ be a positive integer. In how many ways can an $n\times n$ table be filled with integers from $0$ to $5$ such that
a) the sum of each row is divisible by $2$ and the sum of each column is divisible by $3$;
b) the sum of each row is divisible by $2$, the sum of each column is divisible by $3$ and the sum of each of the two diagonals is divisible by $6$?

eet.2022.0.2.reviewed_statement:
Let $p$ be a fixed prime number. Juku and Miku play the following game. One of the players chooses a natural number $a$ such that $a>1$ and $a$ is not divisible by $p$, his opponent chooses any natural number $n$ such that $n>1$. Miku wins if the natural number written as $n$ ones in the positional numeral system with radix $a$ is divisible by $p$, otherwise Juku wins. Which player has a winning strategy if:
(a) Juku chooses the number $a$, tells it to Miku and then Miku chooses the number $n$;
(b) Juku chooses the number $n$, tells it to Miku and then Miku chooses the number $a$?

eet.2024.0.3.reviewed_statement:
How many integers $k$ are there for which $1\le k\le n$ and the sum $1+2+\dots+k$ is divisible by $n$, if
(a) $n=2023^{2024}$;
(b) $n=2024^{2023}$?

pt.2011.1.3.reviewed_statement:
A set of $n$ lamps, numbered from $1$ to $n$, are initially off. At each moment, it is possible to perform one of the following operations:

• change the state of lamp $1$;
• change the state of lamp $2$, provided lamp $1$ is on;
• change the state of lamp $k > 2$, provided lamp $k-1$ is on and all the lamps $1, \dots, k-2$ are off.

Show that it is possible, after a certain number of operations, to have only lamp $n$ on.
